// KQL for this exercise's Application Insights resource.
// Run these in the Azure Portal: App Insights resource -> Logs.
//
// A real deployment did briefly exist this session (see ../README.md), and
// scripts/verify-and-save-function.sh started running against it — but every resource was deleted
// before the script reached this query, so it has still never actually executed against live data.
// Written and checked for correct KQL syntax against the real, standard `requests` table schema
// (same one used in Day 4 Piece 5's queries.kql), not copied blind from the exercise.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Exercise deliverable, verbatim: count/p50/p99 duration per endpoint, last 30 minutes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
requests
| where timestamp > ago(30m)
| summarize count(), p50=percentile(duration, 50), p99=percentile(duration, 99) by name
| order by p99 desc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same query, saved as a reusable function. This is the actual mechanism behind the Logs
// tab's "Save > Save as function" button: the portal wraps whatever query you're looking at in a
// named `let` binding with your chosen name, stored against the workspace, callable later like
// any other table. Doing it explicitly here (rather than only through the UI) means this is
// version-controlled and reviewable, not just a click I can't screenshot.
//
// `lookback` defaults to 30m (matching the exercise), but can be overridden per call —
// EndpointLatencySummary(1h) for the last hour, EndpointLatencySummary(7d) for the last week.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
let EndpointLatencySummary = (lookback:timespan=30m) {
    requests
    | where timestamp > ago(lookback)
    | summarize
        RequestCount = count(),
        p50 = percentile(duration, 50),
        p99 = percentile(duration, 99)
        by name
    | order by p99 desc
};
EndpointLatencySummary()

// To actually save this as a workspace function once a real App Insights resource exists:
//   1. Paste the `let EndpointLatencySummary = ...` block (without the trailing call) into Logs.
//   2. Run it once so the portal can parse it, then click Save -> Save as function.
//   3. Name it "EndpointLatencySummary", give it the category "Performance" (or your preference).
//   4. From then on, `EndpointLatencySummary()` or `EndpointLatencySummary(1h)` works from any
//      query in this workspace, including from other saved queries, workbooks, or alert rules.
